An exact retained case for this issue's legacy-association diagnostic acceptance is pointer-field `WithEnum`:

```go
type Level int
const ( Low Level = iota; High )
type Thing struct { PL *Level `json:"pl"` }

var _ = jsonschema.NewJSONSchemaMethod(
    Thing.Schema,
    jsonschema.WithEnum(Thing{}.PL),
)
```

On `v1.0.0-rc.4`, schema-only generation exits successfully but silently emits `"pl":{"type":"integer"}` with no enum. Adding `--typescript ts` rejects the same declaration with `field Thing.PL enum registration requires a direct named enum type`.

The schema path silently skips the option when its render type is not `*dst.Ident` in [`renderStructField`](https://github.com/tylergannon/go-gen-jsonschema/blob/b2fd7ee189527c2cee0314614b75528e771bd15b/internal/builder/gen_schema.go#L1224-L1229); TypeScript lowering diagnoses it in [`fieldValue`](https://github.com/tylergannon/go-gen-jsonschema/blob/b2fd7ee189527c2cee0314614b75528e771bd15b/internal/builder/typegrammar.go#L342-L348).

This is pre-existing schema behavior exposed by #76, not a TypeScript artifact disagreement: the TypeScript run fails before output mutation. It fits #73's existing requirement that invalid legacy associations fail with a source-positioned error instead of silently losing options. Minimum acceptance for this case is consistent hard failure; supporting pointer enum fields can remain a separate design choice.
